WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.080 --> 00:00:04.290
Film Courage: Wendy, we have a lot of screenwriters
in their 20’s that watch our videos and

00:00:04.290 --> 00:00:09.000
they're just starting out and they're in an
early stages of their writing careers is there

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:11.969
any advice you can give to them?

00:00:11.969 --> 00:00:13.620
LA4hire’s Wendy Kram: Oh sure, luck.

00:00:13.620 --> 00:00:24.439
But one of the best tips was I heard a female
showrunner say when she came out to LA, she

00:00:24.439 --> 00:00:31.340
spent the first year reading every single
pilot that had been picked up that she could

00:00:31.340 --> 00:00:33.340
get her hands on.

00:00:33.340 --> 00:00:38.230
And also maybe reading pilots of series from
a couple years ago.

00:00:38.230 --> 00:00:45.210
When I said Shakespeare for me was one of
my best teachers.

00:00:45.210 --> 00:00:57.370
Just understanding how well-defined his characters
were, the dialogue and the conflicts and the

00:00:57.370 --> 00:01:00.960
characters needs and wants and what gets in
their way.

00:01:00.960 --> 00:01:07.650
I mean it the stakes, you don't get bigger
than Shakespeare with Macbeth or Hamlet.

00:01:07.650 --> 00:01:15.850
And so there are great teachers and Matthew
Weiner [creator of MAD MEN] you can watch

00:01:15.850 --> 00:01:22.000
MAD MEN absolutely, you can watch a lot of
series but there is something about seeing

00:01:22.000 --> 00:01:23.370
the written word.

00:01:23.370 --> 00:01:29.040
The other thing as an exercise (and I think
this is a very powerful exercise) might not

00:01:29.040 --> 00:01:37.240
seem like fun, but it can be fun is to watch
(I did this actually when I was in my 20’s

00:01:37.240 --> 00:01:42.280
starting out) an OFFICER AND A GENTLEMAN and
this is before you could download scripts

00:01:42.280 --> 00:01:48.640
off the Internet I watched the whole movie
and I wrote down as I was watching it what

00:01:48.640 --> 00:01:52.260
each scene was so I had to pause a lot.

00:01:52.260 --> 00:02:01.670
But what it does is it really helps you understand
and get a feel for rhythm and structure almost

00:02:01.670 --> 00:02:06.380
through osmosis through that process of doing
it and the point of doing it isn't so you

00:02:06.380 --> 00:02:13.920
can go and you're not plagiarizing or copying,
it's to get you to just understand and rhythms

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:21.290
of scenes and how they flow from one to the
next and you can see when you're doing...okay

00:02:21.290 --> 00:02:26.520
what's the Act one, the Act one break usually
it's supposed to be around Page 27 in the

00:02:26.520 --> 00:02:35.990
feature so you see where that kind of flows
when you're copying a script and if you don't

00:02:35.990 --> 00:02:43.820
have to do every single line but okay Richard
Gere's with his father they wake up right

00:02:43.820 --> 00:02:53.740
after they've had an evening of debauchery
and hookers and Richard Gere is now off to

00:02:53.740 --> 00:02:58.470
to training camp in the military then the
next scene and I don't remember exactly but

00:02:58.470 --> 00:03:05.980
let's say the next scene is he's on a bus
and we see exterior there the facility then

00:03:05.980 --> 00:03:10.730
next scene maybe we see Debra Winger as a
waitress I don't remember everything so forgive

00:03:10.730 --> 00:03:18.870
me but it also gets you into the understanding
and the that screenwriter is a wonderful screenwriter

00:03:18.870 --> 00:03:26.209
the it gets you into seeing how when I was
talking about not including the dull bits

00:03:26.209 --> 00:03:30.270
you can see beautiful juxtapositions like
he's off to the military and then maybe the

00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:39.370
next thing he's in line and being asked to
salute and it's just it's a wonderful exercise

00:03:39.370 --> 00:03:46.190
that this woman who has became a successful
writer in television she read pilot so if

00:03:46.190 --> 00:03:51.200
you feel like you're want to be a half-hour
person and if you want to do multicam and

00:03:51.200 --> 00:03:57.010
you feel like NBC CBS ABC style sit comes
or your thing which are multicam versus single

00:03:57.010 --> 00:04:05.300
camera which is more like Aziz Ansari and
the The Marvelous Mrs. Maisel, Master of None

00:04:05.300 --> 00:04:12.870
then get those scripts read them see how they
introduce the characters see how distinct

00:04:12.870 --> 00:04:17.319
all the voices are they're so distinct and
these will be some of your best teachers you.

